## Problem

In \`session-manager.ts\`, the \`list()\` and \`get()\` functions use a hardcoded flat path for \`statSync\` to get file timestamps:

\`\`\`typescript
const metaPath = join(config.dataDir, sid);  // line ~437, ~470
const stats = statSync(metaPath);
\`\`\`

But \`ao spawn\` writes metadata to \`{dataDir}/{projectId}-sessions/{sessionId}\` (a subdirectory). The \`statSync\` call throws (caught silently), so \`createdAt\` and \`lastActivityAt\` always fall back to \`new Date()\` — sessions always appear to have just been created.

## Impact

- Session cards in the dashboard show incorrect timestamps
- \`lastActivityAt\` is always "just now" regardless of when the session actually ran
- Sorting by activity time is meaningless

## Fix

Use \`resolveMetadataPath()\` (already exists in \`metadata.ts\`) instead of the hardcoded \`join()\`:

\`\`\`typescript
const metaPath = resolveMetadataPath(config.dataDir, sid);
if (metaPath) {
  const stats = statSync(metaPath);
  createdAt = stats.birthtime;
  modifiedAt = stats.mtime;
}
\`\`\`

## Problem

Tracked in #73: when \`ao start\` launches the Next.js dashboard, the web server calls \`loadConfig()\` from its own CWD (\`packages/web\`). \`findConfigFile()\` doesn't find the project root yaml there, so it falls through to \`~/.agent-orchestrator.yaml\` — which may be stale or point to a different \`dataDir\`.

## Proposed fix

When \`ao start\` resolves the config path (before spawning Next.js), pass it to the web server via an environment variable:

\`\`\`typescript
// in ao start command
const configPath = findConfigFile(projectDir);
process.env.AO_CONFIG_PATH = configPath;
// then spawn Next.js
\`\`\`

\`\`\`typescript
// in packages/web/src/lib/services.ts
const config = loadConfig(process.env.AO_CONFIG_PATH);
\`\`\`

This is the minimal, safe fix. It ensures the dashboard always uses the same config file that \`ao start\` itself resolved — regardless of CWD or home directory fallbacks.

## Alternative

Implement directory traversal in \`findConfigFile()\` (walk from CWD up to \`/\`). This is more complete but changes semantics for all callers.

## Summary

The lifecycle manager's `determineStatus()` gates all PR checks on `session.pr` being set:

```typescript
if (session.pr && scm) {
  // CI monitoring, review detection, mergeability — ALL skipped if session.pr is null
}
```

For Claude Code, `session.pr` is auto-populated via PostToolUse hooks. But agents without auto-hook systems (Codex, Aider, OpenCode) may never write `pr=<url>` to metadata, causing the entire PR state machine to be skipped — no CI monitoring, no review detection, no notifications.

## Current State

- `session.pr` is null → lifecycle manager skips PR checks entirely
- Agent created a PR but metadata was never updated
- Dashboard shows no PR card, no CI status, no reviews
- Notifications for CI failures and review comments never fire

## Proposed Implementation

Add a branch-based PR detection step before the PR state check. The SCM interface already has `detectPR(session, project)` which queries `gh pr list --head <branch>`:

```typescript
// Before checking PR state, auto-detect PR by branch if metadata.pr is missing
if (!session.pr && scm && session.branch) {
  const detectedPR = await scm.detectPR(session, project);
  if (detectedPR) {
    session.pr = detectedPR;
    // Persist to metadata so subsequent polls don't need to re-query
    updateMetadata(sessionsDir, session.id, {
      pr: detectedPR.url,
      status: "pr_open",
    });
  }
}
```

Once detected, the PR URL is persisted to metadata so future poll cycles don't re-query GitHub.

## Impact

This is agent-agnostic — benefits every agent that lacks auto-hook systems:
- **Codex**: no PostToolUse hooks
- **Aider**: no hook system
- **OpenCode**: no hook system
- **Future agents**: automatic support

## Files Changed

- `packages/core/src/lifecycle-manager.ts` — `determineStatus()`

## Related

- #149 — Codex PATH-based shell wrappers (primary metadata mechanism)
- #17 — Codex: implement getSessionInfo and richer activity detection

## Problem

When spawning a session with `ao spawn <project> --agent codex` (or any agent), the system validates that the **plugin** is registered in the plugin registry — but does NOT verify that the actual **CLI binary** (e.g. `codex`, `claude`, `aider`) is installed on the machine.

Current validation in `session-manager.ts`:
```typescript
if (spawnConfig.agent) {
  const overrideAgent = registry.get<Agent>("agent", spawnConfig.agent);
  if (!overrideAgent) {
    throw new Error(`Agent plugin '${spawnConfig.agent}' not found`);
  }
  plugins.agent = overrideAgent;
}
```

This checks plugin registration but not binary availability. If the plugin exists but the CLI is missing, the session spawns, tmux creates a pane, and the command fails silently with "command not found" inside the terminal. The user sees a "Session created" success message but the agent never starts.

This affects **all agent plugins** (claude-code, codex, aider, opencode) — not just Codex.

## Proposed Solution

Add a pre-flight binary check in the spawn flow, before creating any resources (worktree, tmux session, metadata).

### Option A: Agent interface method (recommended)

Add an optional `getBinaryName(): string` method to the `Agent` interface:

```typescript
interface Agent {
  // ... existing methods
  /** Return the CLI binary name to verify it's installed (e.g. "codex", "claude") */
  getBinaryName?(): string;
}
```

In `session-manager.ts` spawn(), after plugin resolution:

```typescript
const binaryName = plugins.agent.getBinaryName?.();
if (binaryName) {
  const { stdout } = await execFileAsync("which", [binaryName], { timeout: 5_000 }).catch(() => ({ stdout: "" }));
  if (!stdout.trim()) {
    throw new Error(
      `Agent CLI '${binaryName}' not found in PATH. Install it before spawning a ${plugins.agent.name} session.`
    );
  }
}
```

Each plugin implements it trivially:
- `agent-codex`: returns `"codex"`
- `agent-claude-code`: returns `"claude"`
- `agent-aider`: returns `"aider"`
- `agent-opencode`: returns `"opencode"`

### Option B: Parse from launch command

Extract the binary name from `getLaunchCommand()` output (first token), then run `which` on it. Avoids adding a new interface method but is fragile if the command format changes.

## Files to modify

- `packages/core/src/types.ts` — add optional `getBinaryName?(): string` to `Agent` interface
- `packages/core/src/session-manager.ts` — add pre-flight check in `spawn()`
- `packages/plugins/agent-codex/src/index.ts` — implement `getBinaryName()`
- `packages/plugins/agent-claude-code/src/index.ts` — implement `getBinaryName()`
- `packages/plugins/agent-aider/src/index.ts` — implement `getBinaryName()`
- `packages/plugins/agent-opencode/src/index.ts` — implement `getBinaryName()`

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `ao spawn project --agent codex` fails fast with a clear error if `codex` CLI is not installed
- [ ] Error message suggests how to install the missing CLI
- [ ] Check runs before any resource creation (no orphaned worktrees/tmux sessions)
- [ ] Existing agents without `getBinaryName()` skip the check gracefully (optional method)
- [ ] Works on macOS and Linux (`which` or `command -v`)

## Problem

When spawning a session with a bare issue number:
```
ao spawn myproject 42
ao spawn myproject "#42"
```

The GitHub tracker plugin stores the raw number as `issueUrl` (e.g. `"42"`) rather than a full `https://` URL. `enrichSessionIssue()` then passes this value directly to `tracker.issueLabel()`, which tries to parse it as a URL — producing a broken label like `"42"` or splitting on `/` and returning the last segment, instead of `"#42"`.

The issue link in the dashboard SessionCard also renders broken (or missing) because the URL is not navigable.

## Fix

In `enrichSessionIssue()`, before calling `issueLabel`, check whether `issueUrl` starts with `"http"`. If not, use `tracker.issueUrl()` to construct the canonical URL first.

```ts
if (!dashboard.issueUrl.startsWith("http") && tracker.issueUrl) {
  try {
    dashboard.issueUrl = tracker.issueUrl(dashboard.issueUrl, project);
  } catch {
    // Leave as-is if construction fails
  }
}
```

This makes the GitHub tracker consistent with how the Linear tracker already works — Linear always stores full URLs, so it was never affected.

## Testing

- `ao spawn <project> 42` → issue link in dashboard shows `#42` and navigates correctly
- `ao spawn <project> "#42"` → same
- Linear sessions unaffected (their `issueUrl` already starts with `https://`)

## Scope

Single file, 11 lines added. No behavior change for sessions that already store full URLs.

## Summary

Improve the first-run experience so a user who clones the repo and runs `bash scripts/setup.sh` has everything validated and set up without hitting silent failures downstream.

## Changes needed

### 1. `scripts/setup.sh` — comprehensive prereq validation

Replace the current minimal script with one that:

- **Hard stops** (exit 1) for missing hard requirements:
  - Node.js < 20 → print install link
  - git < 2.25 → print upgrade link (required for worktree support)

- **Soft warns + optional interactive fix** for:
  - tmux not found → offer `brew install tmux` interactively
  - gh not authenticated → offer `gh auth login` interactively
  - claude CLI not found → print install command

- Uses `corepack enable && corepack prepare --activate` instead of `npm install -g pnpm` so the version in `package.json#packageManager` is enforced

- After `npm link`, verifies `ao` is actually in `$PATH`. If not, prints the exact `export PATH=...` line to add to shell profile

- Updated next-steps output:
  ```
  1. cd /path/to/your-project
  2. ao init --auto
  3. ao start
  ```

### 2. `packages/cli/src/commands/start.ts` — pre-flight before starting

After `loadConfig()` succeeds, before spawning anything:

```typescript
// check dashboard port is free
const portFree = await isPortAvailable(port);
if (!portFree) {
  console.error(`Port ${port} already in use. Free it or change 'port' in agent-orchestrator.yaml`);
  process.exit(1);
}

// check packages are built
const webNext = resolve(findWebDir(), ".next/BUILD_ID");
if (!existsSync(webNext)) {
  console.error("Packages not built. Run: pnpm build");
  process.exit(1);
}
```

### 3. `packages/cli/src/commands/spawn.ts` — pre-flight before spawning

At the start of `spawnSession()`:

```typescript
// tmux check (default runtime)
try {
  await execFileAsync("tmux", ["-V"], { timeout: 3000 });
} catch {
  throw new Error("tmux not installed. Install: brew install tmux");
}

// gh auth check (only for github tracker)
if (project?.tracker?.plugin === "github") {
  try {
    await execFileAsync("gh", ["auth", "status"], { timeout: 5000 });
  } catch {
    throw new Error("GitHub CLI not authenticated. Run: gh auth login");
  }
}
```

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `bash scripts/setup.sh` on a machine missing tmux/gh-auth prompts the user and offers to fix interactively
- [ ] `bash scripts/setup.sh` on Node 18 exits immediately with a clear error
- [ ] `ao start` with port already in use prints a clear error instead of a Next.js crash
- [ ] `ao start` without built packages prints "run pnpm build" instead of a module resolution error
- [ ] `ao spawn` without tmux installed prints a clear error with install instruction
- [ ] All existing tests pass

Closes #257

## Problem

The current code uses a hardcoded `setTimeout(resolve, 5_000)` before sending the initial prompt to a `post-launch` agent. This has two failure modes:

1. **Too slow** — on fast machines, the agent is ready well before 5s, wasting time
2. **Too fast** — on slow machines or under load, 5s may not be enough and the prompt is sent before the agent is ready to receive it

## Solution

Replace the fixed sleep with a polling loop that delegates readiness detection to the agent plugin:

- Polls every **500ms**, timeout of **30 seconds** (sends anyway as best-effort if reached)
- Uses `plugins.agent.detectActivity(output)` — the existing `Agent` interface method — instead of an inline regex, so detection logic stays in one place (the plugin)
- Guards against empty output (`output.trim()`) since `detectActivity` returns `"idle"` for empty strings, which would otherwise trigger a false positive on the very first poll

```typescript
const POLL_INTERVAL_MS = 500;
const TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;
const deadline = Date.now() + TIMEOUT_MS;
let ready = false;
while (Date.now() < deadline) {
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, POLL_INTERVAL_MS));
  const output = await plugins.runtime.getOutput(handle, 10);
  if (output.trim() && plugins.agent.detectActivity(output) === "idle") {
    ready = true;
    break;
  }
}
await plugins.runtime.sendMessage(handle, agentLaunchConfig.prompt);
```

## Changes after review

- **Replaced inline regex** (`/[❯>]\s*$/` on full output) with `plugins.agent.detectActivity(output) === "idle"` — avoids duplicating the detection logic that already exists in the agent plugin, and uses the correct last-line-only check
- **Added empty output guard** — `detectActivity("")` returns `"idle"`, so without `output.trim()` the loop would exit after 500ms on an empty terminal buffer (worse than the original 5s sleep)
- **Updated tests** — fixed 3 tests written for the old 5s sleep, added 2 new regression tests:
  - _"waits until agent signals ready"_ — verifies empty polls are skipped, prompt sent on the poll that finds real output
  - _"does not treat empty terminal output as agent ready"_ — direct guard for the `output.trim()` fix

## Test plan

- [x] Existing post-launch prompt tests updated and passing (82/82)
- [ ] Spawn a new session with an initial prompt — verify the prompt is delivered promptly once the agent is ready
- [ ] Verify behaviour on a slow start (e.g. with a large project) — prompt should still be delivered within 30s
- [ ] Confirm no regression for sessions without an initial prompt (code path is unchanged)

