## Symptom

After all OpenCode AO sessions in a project transition to a terminal status (`killed`, `done`, `merged`, `terminated`, `cleanup`), the dashboard still spawns one fresh `opencode session list --format json` subprocess per `list()` call (every 5 s SSE refresh). Each spawn is a Node + bun process pair that uses ~70–400 MB RSS and takes longer than the 5 s refresh interval to complete, so the queue grows without bound.

Reproduced live: 6 sessions all in `status=killed` on disk, dashboard idle, ~99 in-flight `opencode` subprocesses accumulated within minutes.

This is **distinct from #1118**:
- #1118 is about the per-call duplication inside the plugin (`getActivityState` and `getSessionInfo` each spawn their own `opencode session list`).
- This issue is about the **outer** spawn in core that fires for sessions we know are terminal.

Both bugs compound: even after #1118 lands, this one will still spawn 1 subprocess per refresh forever as long as a single killed opencode session lives in the project sessions directory.

## Root cause

Two interacting design choices in `packages/core/src/session-manager.ts`:

### 1. `loadActiveSessionRecords` is not actually \"active\"

`packages/core/src/session-manager.ts:493-512` — despite the name, this function returns **every** metadata file in `sessionsDir`. There is no status filter. Killed, done, merged, terminated, and cleanup sessions are all returned.

```ts
function loadActiveSessionRecords(project: ProjectConfig): ActiveSessionRecord[] {
  const sessionsDir = getProjectSessionsDir(project);
  if (!existsSync(sessionsDir)) return [];

  const records = listMetadata(sessionsDir).flatMap((sessionName) => {
    const raw = readMetadataRaw(sessionsDir, sessionName);
    if (!raw) return [];
    // ... no status filter ...
    return [{ sessionName, raw, modifiedAt } satisfies ActiveSessionRecord];
  });

  return repairSessionMetadataOnRead(sessionsDir, records, project.sessionPrefix);
}
```

### 2. `list()` eagerly kicks off the OpenCode session list promise for terminal sessions too

`packages/core/src/session-manager.ts:1542-1545`:

```ts
const sessionListPromise =
  effectiveAgentName === \"opencode\"
    ? (openCodeSessionListPromise ??= fetchOpenCodeSessionList())
    : undefined;
```

This fires `fetchOpenCodeSessionList()` (which spawns `opencode session list --format json` — see `session-manager.ts:111-141`) for **any** session whose effective agent is `opencode`, regardless of status. The terminal-status early return only fires later inside `enrichSessionWithRuntimeState` (`session-manager.ts:878-882`):

```ts
async function enrichSessionWithRuntimeState(...) {
  // Skip all subprocess/IO work for sessions already known to be terminal.
  if (TERMINAL_SESSION_STATUSES.has(session.status)) {
    session.activity = \"exited\";
    return;
  }
  // ...
}
```

…but by that point the subprocess has already been started and `ensureOpenCodeSessionMapping` (`session-manager.ts:772-791`) has already awaited the promise.

The combination means: **one terminal opencode session anywhere in the project is enough to spawn `opencode session list` on every refresh forever**.

## Reproduction

1. Spawn an AO session with `--agent opencode`.
2. `ao session kill` it (or let it merge / fail / get cleaned up).
3. Leave the dashboard running.
4. Watch: `watch -n2 'pgrep -afc \"opencode session list\"'`

Expected: 0 (no live opencode sessions — nothing to query)
Actual: count grows linearly until subprocesses start to time out and fail

## Proposed fix

Two layered fixes — both small, both independently valuable.

### Fix A — filter terminal statuses in `loadActiveSessionRecords` (or in `list()`)

The cheapest change. In `list()` at `session-manager.ts:1521`, skip building the openCode promise when all candidate opencode sessions are terminal:

```ts
const TERMINAL_SESSION_STATUSES = new Set([\"killed\", \"done\", \"merged\", \"terminated\", \"cleanup\"]);

const hasLiveOpenCodeSession = allSessions.some(({ raw, projectId: pid }) => {
  const project = config.projects[pid];
  if (!project) return false;
  const selection = resolveSelectionForSession(project, raw.sessionName ?? \"\", raw);
  if (selection.agentName !== \"opencode\") return false;
  return !TERMINAL_SESSION_STATUSES.has(raw[\"status\"] ?? \"\");
});

let openCodeSessionListPromise: Promise<OpenCodeSessionListEntry[]> | undefined =
  hasLiveOpenCodeSession ? undefined : Promise.resolve([]);
```

The key change: pre-resolve the promise to `[]` when there are no live opencode sessions, so the lazy `??=` initializer never spawns a subprocess.

Even better: refactor so the promise is genuinely lazy — only created the first time `ensureOpenCodeSessionMapping` actually needs it for a non-terminal session.

### Fix B — lazy promise creation inside the per-session task

Move the `(openCodeSessionListPromise ??= fetchOpenCodeSessionList())` initializer **into** `ensureOpenCodeSessionMapping`, after the existing `effectiveAgentName !== \"opencode\"` check and after a new terminal-status check:

```ts
async function ensureOpenCodeSessionMapping(
  session: Session,
  ...
): Promise<void> {
  if (effectiveAgentName !== \"opencode\") return;
  if (TERMINAL_SESSION_STATUSES.has(session.status)) return;  // NEW
  if (asValidOpenCodeSessionId(session.metadata[\"opencodeSessionId\"])) return;
  // ... only now does the caller's lazy promise get awaited
}
```

Combined with a thunk passed in from `list()` (`() => fetchOpenCodeSessionList()` memoized) instead of an eagerly-started promise, the subprocess is only spawned when there is actually live opencode work to query.

