- #1744 — Windows desktop notifications not working (other Windows-specific bug)

## Problem

`sm.list()` has no concurrency guard. If N callers invoke it simultaneously, N independent full enrichment runs execute in parallel — each spawning its own set of tmux subprocesses, JSONL reads, and GitHub API calls.

With 83 sessions and 5 concurrent users each polling `?fresh=true` every 2s:
- 5 × 2 requests × 83 tmux probes = **830 concurrent subprocesses** every 2 seconds
- Fresh p50 degrades from 672ms (1 user) to **4011ms** (5 users)

## Note on 1-user scenarios

For the primary local-first use case (1 user), this is not the dominant issue — concurrent calls don't arise. This matters most when multiple browser tabs are open or multiple team members access the same AO instance.

## Fix

Add in-flight deduplication: if `list()` is already running, return the same `Promise<Session[]>` rather than starting a new enrichment run.

```ts
let listInFlight: Promise<Session[]> | null = null;

async function list(projectId?: string): Promise<Session[]> {
  if (!listInFlight) {
    listInFlight = doList().finally(() => { listInFlight = null; });
  }
  const sessions = await listInFlight;
  return projectId ? sessions.filter(...) : sessions;
}
```

This collapses N concurrent `sm.list()` calls into 1 enrichment run. Combined with fixing #2017 (removing `?fresh=true` from the polling loop) and #2018 (batching tmux probes), concurrent-user performance improves dramatically.

## Related
- #2017 — removes the primary source of concurrent fresh calls
- #2018 — reduces per-call cost of sm.list()

